{"body": "Slope is change in y divided by change in x. Distance squares both coordinate changes before taking a square root. A midpoint averages the x-coordinates and the y-coordinates separately.", "content_hash": "sha256:20c84023032987cd6a71a832802a494bbf11b6e249ce21598704dccdea83ffd7", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.coordinate_geometry.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.coordinate_geometry.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_sign_error", "distance_formula_error", "midpoint_formula_error"], "target_skill": "coordinate_geometry", "target_subskill": "slope_distance", "title": "Match Each Coordinate Formula to Its Job", "version": 4}
{"body": "From (1, 2) to (4, 6), slope = (6 - 2)/(4 - 1) = 4/3 and distance = √(3² + 4²) = 5. The midpoint is ((1 + 4)/2, (2 + 6)/2) = (5/2, 4). For y = mx + b through (4, 6), substitute x=4 and y=6 before isolating b; keeping coordinate roles explicit prevents substitution and arithmetic errors.", "content_hash": "sha256:89d535e92b9959579c43db142357346e2aac524189bffaa420a2c5b3bbd5886d", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.coordinate_geometry.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.coordinate_geometry.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_sign_error", "distance_formula_error", "midpoint_formula_error", "input_substitution", "arithmetic_error"], "target_skill": "coordinate_geometry", "target_subskill": "slope_distance", "title": "Slope, Distance, and Midpoint — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "With the same base, multiply by adding exponents and divide by subtracting exponents. A negative exponent means reciprocal. For radicals, pull out only complete square factors.", "content_hash": "sha256:ad99735baa4a80ed4439c0c52c0ca527ced2f36e33df7fd007efe1aa44929568", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.exponents_radicals.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.exponents_radicals.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["exponent_rule_confusion", "negative_exponent_error", "radical_simplification_error"], "target_skill": "exponents_radicals", "target_subskill": "apply_exponent_rules", "title": "Choose the Exponent Rule by Operation", "version": 4}
{"body": "x⁵/x² = x³ because 5 - 2 = 3. Also, √72 = √(36·2) = 6√2. The first step uses quotient exponents; the second extracts the largest square factor. For a negative exponent, 1/x³ = x⁻³: the reciprocal changes the exponent's sign.", "content_hash": "sha256:f9d900dd8dbb4ab425c10226a3f3d23e579924c6df23ba900b19a4ff10311b81", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.exponents_radicals.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.exponents_radicals.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["exponent_rule_confusion", "negative_exponent_error", "radical_simplification_error"], "target_skill": "exponents_radicals", "target_subskill": "apply_exponent_rules", "title": "Exponent Rules and Radicals — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "Linear models have the form f(x) = mx + b. The slope m is the rate of change and b is the starting value.", "content_hash": "sha256:b94218a7970009095985e6874c014bae97c3ef5bae3b1a5b8d06dc2f608bc989", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.functions_models.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.functions_models.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_intercept_swap", "input_substitution"], "target_skill": "functions_models", "target_subskill": "algebraic_models", "title": "Modeling Situations with Functions", "version": 4}
{"body": "To evaluate a function, replace x with the given value everywhere it appears, then simplify. For f(x) = 7x - 6, f(-4) = 7(-4) - 6 = -34.", "content_hash": "sha256:e9791621112a5975b30049b3c01b8b5dccf32a862c848cd17a38f268bd223f4a", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.functions_models.micro_lesson.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.functions_models.micro_lesson.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_intercept_swap", "input_substitution"], "target_skill": "functions_models", "target_subskill": "function_evaluation", "title": "Evaluating Functions by Substitution", "version": 4}
{"body": "For f(x) = 2x + 3, f(5) = 2(5) + 3 = 13.", "content_hash": "sha256:e654290b0564d1a647ad875b58aedd523b0735a6c1fb60f9dbe076da9c5d4b3a", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.functions_models.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.functions_models.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_intercept_swap", "input_substitution"], "target_skill": "functions_models", "target_subskill": "function_evaluation", "title": "Function Evaluation — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "For f(x) = 7x - 6, f(-4) = 7(-4) - 6 = -34.", "content_hash": "sha256:ae11eda8237a15f50a6c197430d0fc5007b96a4f260e1854c795444a8860261a", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.functions_models.worked_example.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.functions_models.worked_example.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["slope_intercept_swap", "input_substitution"], "target_skill": "functions_models", "target_subskill": "function_evaluation", "title": "Evaluating at a Negative Value — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "Solve an inequality as you would an equation, but reverse the inequality sign when multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number. A strict sign (< or >) excludes the boundary; an inclusive sign (≤ or ≥) includes it.", "content_hash": "sha256:a7dec022b2014d350955b73ad753559a4bae810b85b59e66ad198450e673fcf0", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.inequalities.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.inequalities.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["inequality_sign_flip", "boundary_inclusion_error"], "target_skill": "inequalities", "target_subskill": "solve_inequalities", "title": "Keep the Inequality Direction Honest", "version": 4}
{"body": "Solve -3x + 5 ≤ -7. Subtract 5 to get -3x ≤ -12. Divide by -3 and reverse the sign: x ≥ 4. This uses the same inverse operation on both sides; because 4 satisfies the original inequality, the boundary is included.", "content_hash": "sha256:8347f0abcfe78c41dbe52cacf265166fdf4e3c475a4a2ffa6732d8b9b52a7c4c", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.inequalities.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.inequalities.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["inequality_sign_flip", "boundary_inclusion_error", "inverse_operation_error", "arithmetic_error"], "target_skill": "inequalities", "target_subskill": "solve_inequalities", "title": "Negative-Coefficient Inequality — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "To solve ax + b = c, isolate the variable term by subtracting b from both sides, then divide both sides by a. Check by substituting the solution back into the original equation.", "content_hash": "sha256:462817e772e6a3ba45bf811742a0f2526cdbcb674b91740e45c1ce13fbe51940", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.linear_equations.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.linear_equations.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "linear_equations", "target_subskill": "isolate_variables", "title": "Solving Linear Equations", "version": 4}
{"body": "A sign error happens when a term crosses the equals sign with the wrong sign. For 8x - 1 = 87, add 1 to both sides so the left side becomes 8x only: 8x = 88, not 8x = 86. Always state which inverse operation you apply before rewriting the line.", "content_hash": "sha256:30eb2a008f0f86b9a3e38f52df1cac089dec4380c17134673dec679e6971ca70", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.linear_equations.micro_lesson.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.linear_equations.micro_lesson.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "linear_equations", "target_subskill": "isolate_variables", "title": "Avoiding Sign Errors When Isolating", "version": 4}
{"body": "For 3x + 5 = 17: subtract 5 from both sides to get 3x = 12, then x = 4.", "content_hash": "sha256:aba7ae72c7d20e6ead22395cd8854162e9d200b75449fec5dfd17fecd0c6ff49", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.linear_equations.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.linear_equations.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "linear_equations", "target_subskill": "isolate_variables", "title": "Solving a Linear Equation — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "For 8x - 1 = 87: add 1 to both sides to get 8x = 88, then x = 11.", "content_hash": "sha256:27719e73f178459f0e3e9c1098ea4cf2bb555fa650884d4ce3f47876f5679ab8", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.linear_equations.worked_example.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.linear_equations.worked_example.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "linear_equations", "target_subskill": "isolate_variables", "title": "Solving with a Negative Constant — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "If a quadratic factors as (x - p)(x - q) = 0, the zero-product rule gives x = p or x = q. Track the signs inside each factor and keep both roots unless the problem context rules one out.", "content_hash": "sha256:de801473c9a3e5992b3480661a36dce62e99c2e1023c76ec9599a348b53f86fa", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.quadratic_equations.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.quadratic_equations.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["factoring_sign_error", "missing_second_root", "arithmetic_error"], "target_skill": "quadratic_equations", "target_subskill": "factor_quadratics", "title": "Read Both Roots from the Factors", "version": 4}
{"body": "For x² - 7x + 12 = 0, factor to (x - 3)(x - 4) = 0. Therefore x = 3 or x = 4. Checking both values in the original equation confirms both roots.", "content_hash": "sha256:e4fce758f78c858d9fea135767dc1a092d9f6283601fcca76e04884a16e80745", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.quadratic_equations.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.quadratic_equations.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["factoring_sign_error", "missing_second_root", "arithmetic_error"], "target_skill": "quadratic_equations", "target_subskill": "factor_quadratics", "title": "Two Roots from One Quadratic — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "A unit rate expresses a quantity per one unit of another quantity. Set up a proportion so the units match, then solve for the unknown.", "content_hash": "sha256:1cac4f734d67331d0f32079ce3fdc52209a231d83011682e2c5853b8603b2f93", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.ratios_percentages.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.ratios_percentages.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["ratio_inversion", "unit_conversion"], "target_skill": "ratios_percentages", "target_subskill": "unit_rates", "title": "Unit Rates and Proportional Reasoning", "version": 4}
{"body": "Keep the same quantity in the numerator of both ratios. If 20 parts take 2 minutes, write 20/2 = 10 parts per minute; in 8 minutes the machine makes 10 * 8 = 80 parts. Swapping the units inverts the ratio.", "content_hash": "sha256:2d022d48c6f41140118f7e9722ac39e86787282fdb3532a948ad32db345a1943", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.ratios_percentages.micro_lesson.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.ratios_percentages.micro_lesson.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["ratio_inversion", "unit_conversion"], "target_skill": "ratios_percentages", "target_subskill": "unit_rates", "title": "Avoiding Ratio Inversion", "version": 4}
{"body": "At 30 miles per 2 hours, the unit rate is 30 / 2 = 15 miles per hour.", "content_hash": "sha256:010e86d6a8cb5846f5264b438ef9e4cdf194d2d333ed449ab7573f6cf41b2ef6", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.ratios_percentages.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.ratios_percentages.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["ratio_inversion", "unit_conversion"], "target_skill": "ratios_percentages", "target_subskill": "unit_rates", "title": "Unit Rate — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "A machine makes 20 parts in 2 minutes: 20 / 2 = 10 parts per minute, so in 8 minutes it makes 10 * 8 = 80 parts.", "content_hash": "sha256:25e27f09df12c23f334e4dc2de965505e08aec4cdbed91db470fda0afff4c494", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.ratios_percentages.worked_example.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.ratios_percentages.worked_example.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["ratio_inversion", "unit_conversion"], "target_skill": "ratios_percentages", "target_subskill": "unit_rates", "title": "Parts per Minute — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "A system of two linear equations can be solved by elimination: multiply one equation so a variable matches, subtract to eliminate it, solve for one variable, then substitute back.", "content_hash": "sha256:d298cb9dae75268c0bb14ebacd31c2f5bcce17debccdab3c1ec0efeffafb7128", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.systems_equations.micro_lesson.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.systems_equations.micro_lesson.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "systems_equations", "target_subskill": "solve_systems", "title": "Solving Systems of Equations", "version": 4}
{"body": "When neither variable has matching coefficients, multiply one equation first so a variable matches, then subtract. For 4x + y = 30 and 4x + 3y = 42 the x coefficients already match: subtract the first equation from the second to get 2y = 12, so y = 6, then x = 6.", "content_hash": "sha256:470f3596c74ce2e78ab8973eee2ae5f2cf40700108e6218b452583194609789b", "content_type": "micro_lesson", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.systems_equations.micro_lesson.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.systems_equations.micro_lesson.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "systems_equations", "target_subskill": "solve_systems", "title": "Eliminating When Coefficients Do Not Match", "version": 4}
{"body": "For x + y = 5 and x - y = 1: adding gives 2x = 6, so x = 3, then y = 2.", "content_hash": "sha256:598ad2a39c3fea43eb91af2de9695fea190ed96862e716061f686cc9bf2bd2e9", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.systems_equations.worked_example.001", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.systems_equations.worked_example.001", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "systems_equations", "target_subskill": "solve_systems", "title": "Solving a System — Worked Example", "version": 4}
{"body": "For 4x + y = 30 and 4x + 3y = 42: subtract to eliminate x, giving 2y = 12, so y = 6, then x = 6.", "content_hash": "sha256:52fc4baff4718cbae125fda958f7a30ab873c31e765e14545aceabf8a0565410", "content_type": "worked_example", "difficulty": 1, "domain": "math", "estimated_seconds": 120, "id": "math.systems_equations.worked_example.002", "license": {"id": "bridgesat_original", "independent_answer_verification": true, "name": "BridgeSAT original content", "recorded_authorship": "ai_assisted_bridgesat"}, "required_prerequisites": ["linear_equations", "integer_operations"], "review_status": "schema_validated", "reviewers": {}, "schema_version": "v1", "source_lineage": {"lineage_id": "bridgesat-original:math.systems_equations.worked_example.002", "role": "concept_source_only", "source_id": "bridgesat_original"}, "target_misconceptions": ["sign_error", "inverse_operation_error"], "target_skill": "systems_equations", "target_subskill": "solve_systems", "title": "System with Matching Coefficient — Worked Example", "version": 4}
